G2532.d:
καί, 
 conj.,  
and 
  I. Copulative. 
  1. Connecting single words; 
  (a) in general: Matt.2:18, 16:1, Mark.2:15, Luke.8:15, Heb.1:1, al. mult.; repeated before each of the terms in a series, Matt.23:23, Luke.14:21, Rom.7:12, 9:4, al.
  (b) connecting numerals (WM, §37, 4): John.2:20, Acts.13:20; 
  (with) joining terms which are not mutually exclusive, as the part with the whole: Matt.8:33, 26:59, Mark.16:17, Acts.5:29, al. 
  2. Connecting clauses and sentences: Matt.3:12, Acts.5:21, al. mult.; esp. 
  (a) where, after the simplicity of the popular language, sentences are paratactically joined (WM, §60, 3; M, Pr., 12; Deiss., LAE, 128ff.): Matt.1:21, 7:25, Mark.9:5, John.10:3, al.; 
  (b) joining affirmative to negative sentences: Luke.3:14, John.4:11, IIIJhn.10; 
  (with) consecutive, and so: Matt.5:1, 23:32, Heb.3:19, al.; after imperatives, Matt.4:19, Luke.7:7, al.; 
  (d) = καίτοι, and yet: Matt.3:14, 6:26, Mark.12:12, Luke.18:7 (Field, Notes, 72), 1 Cor.5:2, al.; 
  (e) beginning an apodosis (= Heb. וְ; so sometimes δέ in cl.), then: Luke.2:21, 7:12, Acts.1:10; beginning a question (WM, §53, 3a): Mark.10:26, Luke.10:29, John.9:36.
  3. Epexegetic, and, and indeed, namely (WM, §53, 3c): Luke.3:18, John.1:16, Acts.23:6, Rom.1:5, 1 Cor.3:5, al.
  4. In transition: Matt.4:23, Mark.5:1, 21, John.1:19, al.; so, Hebraistically, καὶ ἐγένετο (וַי:הִי; also ἐγένετο δέ), Mark.1:9 (cf. Luke.5:1; V. Burton, §§357-60; M, Pr., 14, 16). 
  5. καὶ . . . καί, both . . . and (for τε . . . καί, see: τε); 
  (a) connecting single words: Matt.10:28, Mark.4:41, Rom.11:33, al.; 
  (b) clauses and sentences: Mark.9:13, John.7:28, 1 Cor.1:22, al. 
  II. Adjunctive, also, even, still: Matt.5:39, 40; Mark.2:28, al. mult.; esp. with pron., adv., etc., Matt.20:4, John.7:47, al; ὡς κ., Acts.11:17; καθὼς κ., Rom.15:7; οὑτω κ., Rom.6:11; διὸ κ., Luke.1:35; ὁ κ. (Deiss., BS, 313ff.), Acts.13:9; pleonastically, μετὰ κ.. (Bl., §77, 7; Deiss., BS, 265f,), Phil.4:3; τί κ., 1 Co 15:29; ἀλλὰ κ., Luke.14:22, John.5:18, al.; καίγε (M, Pr., 230; Burton, §437), Acts.17:27; καίπερ, Heb.5:8; κ. ἐάν, see: ἐάν.
  ἐάν, contr. fr. εἰ ἄν, 
 conditional particle, 
representing something as "under certain circumstances actual or liable to happen," but not so definitely expected as in the case of εἰ with ind. (Bl., §65, 4; cf. John.13:17, 1 Cor.7:36), if haply, if; 
  1. with subjc. (cl.); 
  (a) pres.: Matt.6:22, Luke.10:6, John.7:17, Rom.2:25, 26 al.; 
{  (b) aor. (= Lat. fut. pf.): Matt.4:9 16:26 (cf. ptcp. in Luke.9:25; M, Pr., 230), Mark.3:24, Luke.14:34, John.5:43, Rom.7:2, al.; = cl. εἰ, with opt., John.9:22 11:57, Acts.9:2; as Heb. אִם = ὅταν, John.12:32 14:3, I John.2:28 3:2, Heb.3:7" (LXX) . 
  2. C. indic, (as in late writers, fr. Arist. on; see WH, App., 171; VD, MGr. 2, App., §77; Deiss., BS, 201f., LAE, 155, 254; M, Pr., 168, 187; Bl., §65, 4); 
  (a) fut.: Matt.18:19 T, Luke.19:40,  Acts.7:7; 
  (b) pres.: 1 Thess.3:8 (see Milligan, in l.). 
  3. With other particles: ἐ. καί (Bl., §65, 6), Gal.6:1; ἐ. μή (M, Pr., 185, 187; Bl., l.with), with subjc. pres., Matt.10:13, 1 Cor.8:8, Jas.2:17, 1 John.3:21; aor., Matt.6:15, Mark.3:27, John.3:3, Rom.10:15, Gal.1:8 2:16 (see Lft., Ellic., in ll.); ἐ. τε . . . ἐ. τε, [in LXX for אִם . . . אִם, Est.19:13, al.,] Rom.14:8. 
  4. = cl. ἄν (which see) after relat. pronouns and adverbs (Tdf., Pr., 96; WH, App., 173; M, Pr., 42f.; Bl., §26, 4; Mayser, 152f.; Deiss., BS, 202ff.): ὃς ἐ., Matt.5:19, Mark.6:22, 23 Luke.17:32, 1 Cor.6:18, al.; ὅπου ἐ., Matt.8:19; ὁσάκις ἐ., Rev.11:6; οὗ ἐ., 1 Cor.16:6; καθὸ ἐ., 2 Cor.8:12; ὅστις ἐ., Gal.5:10. 
 (AS)

G2563.d:
κάλαμος, -ου, ὁ 
 [in LXX chiefly for קָנֶה, Isa.42:3, Eze.40:3 ff., al. ;] 
a reed: Matt.11:7 12:20" (LXX) Luke.7:24; hence, accusative, to its various uses, 
  (a) a reed-pipe, flute; 
  (b) a reed-staff, staff (cf. 4Ki.18:21, ῥάβδος καλαμίνη): Matt.27:29-30, 48, Mark.15:19, 36; 
  (with) a measuring reed or rod (Ez, l.with): Rev.11:1 21:15-16; 
  (d) a writing reed, a pen: III Jn 13 (cf. Milligan, NTD, 9, 7).†
 (AS)

G2596.d:
κατά (bef. a vowel κατ᾽, καθ᾽; on the frequently neglect of elision, see Tdf., Pr., 95; WH, App., 146a), 
 prep. with genitive, accusative,  
down, downwards. 
  I. C. genitive (WM, §47, k; Bl., §42, 2). 
  1. C. genitive of thing(s), in local sense; 
  (a) down, down from: Matt.8:32, Mark.5:13, Luke.8:33, 1 Cor.11:4; 
  (b) throughout (late usage; Bl, l.with): κ. ὅλης κ.τ.λ., Luke.4:14  23:5, Acts.9:31  10:37; 
  (with) in a peculiar adjectival phrase: ἡ κ. βάθους, deep or extreme poverty, 2 Cor.8:2. 
  2. C. genitive of person(s), usually in hostile sense; 
  (a) against (in cl. only after verbs of speaking, witnessing, etc.): opposite to ὑπέρ, Mark.9:40; μετά, Matt.12:30; after ἐπιθυμεῖν, Gal.5:17; λαλεῖν, Acts.6:13; διδάσκειν, Acts.21:28; ψεύδεσθαι, Jas.3:14; after verbs of accusing, etc., Matt.5:23, Luke.23:14, Rom.8:33, al.; verbs of fighting, prevailing, etc., Matt.10:35, Acts.14:2, 1 Cor.4:6, al.; 
  (b) of swearing, by: όμνυμι κ. (BL, §34, 1), He  6:13,16, cf. Matt.26:63. 
  II. C. accusative (WM, §49d; BL, §42, 2). 
  1. Of motion or direction; 
  (a) through, throughout: Luke.8:39  9:6 10:4, Acts.8:1,  36 al.; 
  (b) to, towards, over against: Luke.10:32 (Field, Notes, 62), Acts.2:1o 16:7, Gal.2:11, Phil.3:14, al.; 
  (with) in adverbial phrases, at, in, by, of: κατ᾽  οἶκον, at home, Acts.2:46; κατ᾽ ἰδίαν (see: ἴδιος); καθ᾽ ἑαυτόν, Acts.28:16, Rom.14:22, Jas.2:17; with pron. of person(s), Acts.17:28  18:15, Rom.1:15, Eph.1:15, al. 
  2. Of time, at, during, about: Acts.8:26  12:1 19:23, Rom.9:9 Heb.1:10, al. 
  3. Distributive; 
  (a) of place: κ. τόποὐς, Matt.24:7, al.; κ. πόλιν, Luke.8:1,  4 al.; κ. ἐκκλησίαν, Acts.14:23. 
  (b) of time: κ. ἔτος, Luke.2:41; ἑορτήν, Matt.27:15, al.; 
  (with) of numbers, etc.: καθ᾽ ἕνα πάντες, 1 Cor.14:31 (on καθ᾽ εἷς, see: εἷς); κ. ἑκατόν, Mark.6:40; κ. μέρος, Heb.9:5; κ. ὄνομα, John.10:3. 
  4. Of fitness, reference, conformity, etc.; 
  (a) in relation to, concerning: Rom.1:3, 4 7:22 9:3, 5, 1 Cor.12:6 10:18, Phil.1:12; κ. πάντα, Acts.17:22, Col.3:20, 22 Heb.2:17 4:15; 
  (b) according to, after, like: Mark.7:5, Luke.2:27, 29 John.7:24 Rom.8:4 14:15, Eph.2:2, Col.2:8, Jas.2:8, al. 
  III. In composition, κ. denotes, 
  1. down, down from (καταβαίνω), etc.), hence, metaphorically; 
  (a) victory or rule over (καταδουλόω, -κυριεύω, etc.); 
  (b) "perfective" action (M, Pr., 111ff.). 
  2. under (κατακαλύπτω), etc.). 
  3. in succession (καθεξῆς). 
  4. after, behind (καταλείπω). 
  5. Hostility, against (καταλαλέω). 
 (AS)

G2634.d:
κατα-κυριεύω 
 [in LXX: Gen.1:28 (כָּבַשׁ), Ps.110:2 Sir.17:4, al. ;] 
to gain or exercise dominion (over); 
  (a) absol. (Arist.); 
  (b) with genitive (Arist., al.): Matt.20:25, Mark.10:42, Acts.19:16, 1 Pet.5:3.†
 SYN.: (κατεξουσιάζω) (see Swete, Mk., l.with)  (AS)

G2675.d:
κατ-αρτίζω 
 [in LXX chiefly in Ps (Ps.8:2 al.; כּוּן, etc.) and Ezra (כְּלַל) ;] 
to render ἄρτιος, i.e. fit, complete; 
  (a) to mend, repair: Matt.4:21, Mark.1:19; 
  (b) to furnish completely, complete, equip, prepare: pass., Luke.6:40, Rom.9:22, Heb.11:3; mid., Matt.21:16 (LXX), Heb.10:5 (LXX); 
  (with) in ethical sense, to prepare, complete, perfect: Gal.6:1 (EV, restore), 1 Pet.5:10; pass., 1 Cor.1:10 (Field, Notes, 167), 2 Cor.13:11, Heb.13:21 (cf. προ-καταρτίζω).†
 (AS)

G2760.d:
κεντυρίων, -ωνος, ὁ (Lat. centurio); 
 a centurion: Mark.15:39, 44-45 (Mt, Lk, use ἑκιτόνταρχος (-ης), q.v).†
 (AS)

G2799.d:
κλαίω 
 [in LXX chiefly for בָּכָה ;] 
of any loud expression of pain or sorrow, esp. for the dead, to weep, lament; 
  (a) intrans.: Mark.5:38-39 14:72 (M, Pr., 131) Mark.16:10, Luke.7:13, 32 7:38 8:52, John.11:31, 33 16:20 20:11, 13 20:15 Acts.9:39 21:13, 1 Cor.7:30, Phil.3:18, Jas.4:9 5:1, Rev.5:5 18:15, 19; πολύ, Rev.5:4; πολλά, Acts.8:24 (WH, mg.); πικρῶς, Matt.26:75, Luke.22:62; opposite to γελάω, Luke.6:21, 25; χαίρω, Rom.12:15; before ἐπί, with accusative, Luke.19:41 23:28, Rev.18:9; 
  (b) trans., with accusative of person(s), to weep or lament for, bewail: Matt.2:18.†
 SYN.: see: δακρύω  (AS)

G2819.d:
κλῆρος, -ου, ὁ 
 [in LXX chiefly for נַחֲלָה גּוֹרָל ;] 
a lot; 
  (a) that which is cast or drawn: Matt.27:35, Mark.15:24, Luke.23:34, John.19:24" (LXX), Acts.1:26; 
  (b) that which is obtained by casting: Acts.1:17 8:21; of a part in the Kingdom of God (cf. Wis 55), Acts.26:18, Col.1:12; pl. of the "cure" of a presbyter, 1 Pet.5:3 (see ICC, in l).†
 (AS)

G2822.d:
κλητός, -ή, -όν
 (καλέω), [in LXX for קָרָא, מִקְרָא, Exod.12:16, al. ;] 
called, invited (as to a banquet, Æsch., 1Sa 1:41, 3Ma.5:14); in NT, always of the Divine call; 
  (a) to some office: κ. ἀπόστολος, Rom.1:1, 1 Cor.1:1; 
  (b) to salvation: Rom.8:28, 1 Cor.1:24, Ju 1; κ. καὶ ἐκλεκτοὶ κ. πιστοί, Rev.17:14; in gosp. (not in pl., see: καλέω) disting. fr.ἐκλεκτός, Matt.20:16 (WH, txt., R, omit), Matt.22:14; κ. Ἰησοῦ Χρ., Rom.1:6; κ. ἅγιοι, saints by calling, Rom.1:7, 1 Cor.1:2.†
 (AS)

G2908.d:
κρείσσων
  (Epic and old Att..) and κρείττων (later Att..), prop. comparat. of κρατύς, strong, but in sense often (as in cl., see LS, see word) as comparat. of ἀγαθός,  [in LXX chiefly for טוֹב ;]  
better; 
  (a) as to ad­vantage or usefulness: 1 Cor.11:17, Heb.11:40 12:24; πολλῷ μᾶλλον κ., Phil.1:23; κρεῖσσον ποιεῖν, 1 Cor.7:38; ἐστιν, before inf., 1 Cor.7:9, 2 Pet.2:21; 
  (b) as to excellence: Heb.1:4 6:9 7:7, 19 7:22 8:6 9:23 10:34 11:16, 35; κ. ἐστι, before inf., 1 Pet.3:17.†
 (AS)

